The problem setup starts from the two pure parental haplotypes (00000 and 11111). Exactly one crossover occurs per meiosis and is confined to one of the four intervals between the five SNPs. Consequently an F3 haplotype is always the product of precisely two ancestral meioses and therefore contains at most two allelic transitions.

All binary strings of length 5 that satisfy this transition bound are reachable:
- 0 transitions: 00000, 11111 (2 strings);
- 1 transition: four possible cut-points, each reachable from either parental background (8 strings);
- 2 transitions: any ordered pair of distinct cut-points, again reachable from either background (C(4,2)×2 = 12 strings).

The total is therefore 2 + 8 + 12 = 22.  

The “single-orientation” clause simply excludes the reciprocal recombinant chromatid that would normally be produced in the same meiosis; because the problem already instructs us to examine only one arbitrarily chosen strand, that clause does not remove any of the 22 sequences enumerated above. Strings containing three or more transitions are unreachable because they would require at least three ancestral crossovers.

Thus exactly 22 distinct sequences are possible on the positive strand in the F3 generation.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {22}

